{"name":"Zen Essence","hex":"#c6bfa7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Zenaga."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of enlightenment, particularly the kind acquired through Zen meditation.","An approach to instruction, understanding, or an activity similarly emphasizing simplicity and intuition rather than conventional thinking or fixation on goals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zen"},{"term":"essence","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inherent nature of a thing or idea.","The true nature of anything, not accidental or illusory."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/essence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zen","blocks":[{"text":"Zen is a Mahayana Buddhist tradition that developed in China during the Tang dynasty by blending Indian Mahayana Buddhism, particularly Yogacara and Madhyamaka philosophies, with Chinese Taoist thought, especially Neo-Daoist. Zen originated as the Chan school or the Buddha-mind school, and later developed into various sub-schools and branches."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Ashtamangala_Lotus.svg/330px-Ashtamangala_Lotus.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen"},{"title":"Essence","blocks":[{"text":"Essence has various meanings and uses for different thinkers and in different contexts. It is used in philosophy and theology as a designation for the property or set of properties or attributes that make an entity the entity it is or, expressed negatively, without which it would lose its identity. Essence is contrasted with accident, which is a property or attribute the entity has accidentally or contingently, but upon which its identity does not depend."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essence"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Eiheiji_gate.jpg/500px-Eiheiji_gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Eiheiji_gate.jpg/1280px-Eiheiji_gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Eiheiji_gate.jpg/500px-Eiheiji_gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eiheiji gate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEiheiji_gate.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg/500px-Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg/1280px-Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg/500px-Kodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kodo Sawaki practicing zazen, his hands make the \"cosmic mudra\" (Jp: hokkai jōin 法界定印), which is common in Japanese Soto Zen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKodo_Sawaki_Zazen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Hsuan_Hua_Hong_Kong_1.jpeg/330px-Hsuan_Hua_Hong_Kong_1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Hsuan_Hua_Hong_Kong_1.jpeg/330px-Hsuan_Hua_Hong_Kong_1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Venerable Hsuan Hua meditating in the lotus position, Hong Kong, 1953","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHsuan_Hua_Hong_Kong_1.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/%E6%9D%B1%E5%B6%BA%E5%9C%93%E6%85%88%E6%89%8B%E6%9B%B8%E7%84%A